<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Only the keys Xcode cannot generate. GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on and merges the
	  rest in, so the usage strings and scene manifest still live in build settings where
	  they were. Arrays of dictionaries have no INFOPLIST_KEY form, which is the whole
	  reason this file exists.
	-->

	<!--
	  **The two standard authenticator schemes, and nothing of our own.**

	  Declaring these is what makes iOS offer OpenFactor when the Camera app or Photos finds a
	  QR code holding one. Without them a setup code scanned outside this app has no route in
	  except retyping it.

	  `otpauth` is one account. `otpauth-migration` is a transfer carrying many, which is what
	  Google Authenticator's export QR encodes. Both go to the same place, because the payload
	  names its own format and the add screen already tells them apart.

	  **Two costs, neither hidden.** A declared scheme is an entry point every app on the
	  device can use; the guard is that a code arriving this way lands on the confirm screen
	  and nothing is ever saved without somebody reading the issuer and name. And an
	  `otpauth://` URL carries the secret in the clear, so accepting one means it passes
	  through the system rather than staying inside this process as it does when our own
	  camera decodes the frame. That is unavoidable if the scheme is supported at all, and it
	  is the reason no scheme of OpenFactor's own exists here: an earlier `openfactor` scheme
	  was removed once nothing could produce it.
	-->
	<!--
	  **One window, deliberately, and App Lock depends on it.**

	  Xcode generates this manifest as `true` for a SwiftUI app, which is what shipped: the app
	  declared multiple scenes on iPhone and iPad while `PrivacyShield` kept a single lock window
	  and a single cover window, both attached to `connectedScenes.first`. On an iPad with two
	  OpenFactor windows open, the second had no cover in the app switcher and no lock over it,
	  which is exactly what `SECURITY.md` and `docs/APP_LOCK.md` promise cannot happen. Found in
	  gate A4 by one engine of three; a second asked the same question and got the answer wrong
	  from the project file rather than the built one.

	  Two ways out. Make the lock and the cover per scene, keyed by scene identity, with
	  lifecycle events routed per scene and teardown on disconnect. Or have one window. The
	  second was chosen: an authenticator is opened to read a code and left, two windows of it
	  serve nobody, and the alternative is permanently more surface in the layer that has
	  produced the most defects in this project.

	  **This costs nothing else on iPad.** Split View and Slide Over beside a *different* app do
	  not require multiple scenes and still work; only a second window of OpenFactor is gone.

	  `PrivacyShield` says out loud that it assumes one scene, and CI fails if this key ever
	  becomes true again, because the assumption is invisible at the point where it would break.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>

	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>dev.openfactor.app.otpauth</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>otpauth</string>
				<string>otpauth-migration</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  This project's own backup, declared so it has a name and an icon in Files and Mail
	  rather than being an unknown blob. Exported rather than imported, because this project
	  defines the format: docs/BACKUP_FORMAT.md is its specification.
	-->
	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>dev.openfactor.backup</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>OpenFactor backup</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>openfactor</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
	  What "Open in OpenFactor" offers, from Files or a mail attachment, so a backup does not
	  have to be routed through the in-app picker.

	  **Aegis is declared as JSON rather than as its own type.** Aegis does not publish a
	  uniform type identifier, and inventing one here would claim ownership of somebody
	  else's format. The importer already sniffs the contents and refuses what it does not
	  recognize, so the plist being broad costs nothing: a JSON file that is not an Aegis
	  vault is turned away by the parser, not by this list.
	-->
	<!--
	  **A copy, not the original.** Apple's upload check flags a CFBundleDocumentTypes
	  declaration that does not answer this, and leaving it unanswered means iOS decides for
	  itself which one OpenFactor gets.

	  NO is right here. The importer reads the bytes and is finished; asking to open in place
	  would be asking for write access to somebody's file for no reason at all, and this app
	  should hold the fewest permissions that do the job.
	-->
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<false/>

	<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>OpenFactor backup</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Owner</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>dev.openfactor.backup</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>Authenticator export</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Alternate</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.json</string>
				<string>public.plain-text</string>
				<string>public.rtf</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!-- Export compliance, answered once here rather than clicked on every upload.

	     The app uses AES-256-GCM, HKDF, HMAC, SHA-256 and P-256 ECDH from CryptoKit, and PBKDF2
	     from CommonCrypto because CryptoKit has no password based derivation. Nothing
	     cryptographic is implemented in this repository except RFC 4226 dynamic truncation,
	     which is arithmetic on an HMAC output and is not encryption.

	     So the app does use encryption, and Xavier's determination is that it qualifies for the
	     exemption for software using only encryption available in Apple's operating systems,
	     with no proprietary or non standard implementation. That is a legal classification and
	     it is his, not this project's tooling's. The facts it rests on are listed in
	     docs/APP_STORE.md and are complete.

	     Recorded here so the answer is versioned with the source and cannot drift from what was
	     decided. If the cryptography above ever changes, this key is part of that change. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
